> jacobian x^2+y, x*y wrt x, y
Jacobian of (x^2 + y, x*y) wrt (x, y): row i = the gradient of function i
  [ 2x   1 ]
  [ y    x ]
  det = 2x^2 - y
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 400 points (max relative difference 3e-13)
> hessian x^2*y + y^2 at 1, 2
Hessian of x^2*y + y^2 (row i, column j: ∂²/∂j∂i):
  [ 2y   2x ]   ∂/∂x
  [ 2x   2  ]   ∂/∂y
  det = 4y - 4x^2
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 400 points (max relative difference 3e-13)
  symmetric (the mixed partials agree) at 100 of 100 sample pairs
  at (x = 1, y = 2):
  [ 4          2          ]
  [ 2          2          ]
  det = 4
  the gradient is not 0 there (largest component 5): not a critical point
> wronskian exp(x), exp(2x)
Wronskian matrix of exp(x), exp(2x) (row k: the k-th derivatives wrt x)
  [ exp(x)   exp(2x)  ]   the functions
  [ exp(x)   2exp(2x) ]   d/dx
  W = det = exp(3x)
  (W equals the numeric determinant at all 200 sample points of [-10, 10])
  check: every entry matches central differences at 402 points (max relative difference 3e-14)
  linearly independent: W(-1/20) = 0.8607079764 != 0, so the functions are independent on every interval containing x = -1/20
> wronskian 1, x, x^2
Wronskian matrix of 1, x, x^2 (row k: the k-th derivatives wrt x)
  [ 1   x   x^2 ]   the functions
  [ 0   1   2x  ]   d/dx
  [ 0   0   2   ]   d²/dx²
  W = det = 2
  (W equals the numeric determinant at all 200 sample points of [-10, 10])
  check: every entry matches central differences at 1206 points (max relative difference 2e-15)
  linearly independent: W(-1/20) = 2 != 0, so the functions are independent on every interval containing x = -1/20
> laplacian x^2 + y^2 + z^2
∇² x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 6
  ∂²/∂x² x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 2
  ∂²/∂y² x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 2
  ∂²/∂z² x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 2
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 600 points (max relative difference 4e-13)
> grad x^2*y*z at 1, 2, 3
∇ x^2*y*z = (2x*y*z, x^2*z, x^2*y)
  ∂/∂x x^2*y*z = 2x*y*z
  ∂/∂y x^2*y*z = x^2*z
  ∂/∂z x^2*y*z = x^2*y
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 300 points (max relative difference 4e-13)
  at (x = 1, y = 2, z = 3): (12, 3, 2), |∇| = 12.52996409
> integrate x^2 wrt x from 0 to 1
∫ x^2 dx
  = x^3/3 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 1] (max difference 1.1e-16)
  from 0 to 1: F(1) - F(0) = 0.333333333333333
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 0.333333333333333, estimated error 3.7e-15
  the two agree to 0
> integrate 1/x wrt x from 1 to 2
∫ 1/x dx
  = log(abs(x)) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [1, 2] (max difference 0)
  from 1 to 2: F(2) - F(1) = 0.693147180559945
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 0.693147180559945, estimated error 7.6e-15
  the two agree to 1.1e-16
> integrate 1/x^2 wrt x from -1 to 1
∫ 1/x^2 dx
  = -1/x + C
  method: partial fractions, Hermite reduction for the repeated factors
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 400 points of [-1, 1] (max difference 0)
  numeric: the integrand is undefined or infinite inside the interval (singular or divergent)
  quasi-Monte Carlo does not settle either: 8 replicates of 4096 Sobol points give 3.81e+04 with a standard error of 6.3e+03: no value (a pole, or an integral that does not exist)
> integrate sin(x)^2 wrt x from 0 to pi
∫ sin(x)^2 dx
  = x/2 - sin(2x)/4 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 3.14159] (max difference 1.1e-16)
  from 0 to 3.14159: F(3.14159) - F(0) = 1.5707963267949
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 1.5707963267949, estimated error 1.7e-14
  the two agree to 0
> antiderivative sin(x)*cos(x)
∫ sin(x)*cos(x) dx
  = -cos(x)^2/2 + C
  method: Bioche: t = cos(x)
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 0)
> closed sum(i,1,n,i)
  note: n is a bound of sum: taken as int in 0..100
sum(i, 1, n, i)
  = n*(n + 1)/2
  method: Faulhaber (polynomial in the index)
  check: equals direct iteration at all 101 points of the domain
> closed sum(i,0,n,2^i)
  note: n is a bound of sum: taken as int in 0..100
sum(i, 0, n, 2^i)
  = 2^(n + 1) - 1
  method: geometric
  check: equals direct iteration at all 62 points of the domain
  but at 1 points the closed form is undefined (an intermediate leaves the 64-bit range) where iteration is not, e.g. n=62
> integrate 1/z around |z|=1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
integral of 1/z around |z| = 1 (counterclockwise):
  = 2pi*i
  numerically: the trapezoid rule on the circle with 1024 and 2048 points (difference 1e-18)
  residue theorem: 2 pi i times the sum of the residues at the 1 pole inside = 2pi*i: agrees
